---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import SubNav from "../../../components/SubNav.astro";
import Section from "../../../components/Section.astro";
import Card from "../../../components/Card.astro";

const placement = `{
  "id": "pl_7QK2",                 // stable for the life of this version
  "block": "wall.stone.2",
  "pos": [12.0, 0.0, 7.5],         // metres, not cells
  "rot": 30.0,                     // degrees, whatever the snap offered
  "parent": null,                  // or another placement id
  "params": { "material": "mossy" }
}`;

const snapTargets = `centre  crates, altars, spawns  the hex it stands in
edge    walls, doors, gates     the line between two hexes
corner  pillars, posts          where three hexes meet
free    set pieces              whatever the author insists on`;
---

<Base title="Dungeon building blocks — Party Dungeon" current="/mechanics/dungeon/">
  <SubNav current="/mechanics/dungeon/" />

  <header>
    <div class="kicker">
      <a href="/mechanics/">Mechanics</a> /
      <a href="/mechanics/dungeon/">Your dungeon</a> / Building blocks
    </div>
    <h1>The hex is a snapping frame, not a storage format.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Hexes make placement quick and rotations tidy. The save file is still a
      list of things at positions, and physics never hears about a hex. For now
      every dungeon is hand-authored.
    </p>
  </header>

  <Section title="What the grid is for">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="decided" title="Hexes snap; they don't store" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          The grid is an authoring aid. A placed object keeps a position and a
          rotation; which hex it clung to is not part of the dungeon.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="30&deg; rotation while placing" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Items turn in 30&deg; steps so they meet a hex edge or sit between two.
          The stored angle is just a number &mdash; nothing quantises it later,
          and nothing in physics rounds it.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Hand-authored first" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          No generator in the first build. The procedural path stays open, but
          nothing has to satisfy it yet, and the editor is the only way a
          dungeon gets made.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="bet" title="Revised: the grid stopped being the model">
        <p>
          The previous pass of this page stored walls as edges between hexes.
          That made the grid authoritative. It isn't &mdash; it's a guide that
          happens to make doors line up.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="There is no room type">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="decided" title="A room is not a building block" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          A room is what you get when walls enclose some floor. Making it a type
          means a block that secretly contains other blocks &mdash; and then two
          ways to say the same thing, which always drift apart.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="A room is a region the editor derives">
        <p>
          Flood-fill the walkable area bounded by walls and closed doors. That
          region is what spawning, lighting, AI and loot budgets refer to. It is
          computed, never stored as a thing you can place.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="A reusable room is a saved arrangement">
        <p>
          The authored <code>boss_room: "ossuary-b"</code> on the data page is a
          fragment: objects with transforms, kept as a document. It is a
          <em>layout</em>, not a type &mdash; reuse without inventing a
          composite.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Nine behaviours">
    <p class="lede" style="font-size:16px; margin-bottom:18px">
      Everything you place is one of these. The type is a behaviour, not a
      theme &mdash; which is why a wooden door and a stone door are the same
      type with different prefabs.
    </p>
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="guess" title="Surface">
        <p>
          The ground: floor, water, pit, void. Owns level changes &mdash; a ramp
          is a surface with a height delta, not a separate kind of thing.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Barrier">
        <p>
          Wall, pillar, fence, window. Refuses passage and sometimes sight.
          Walls snap to hex edges, pillars to corners; physics only sees the
          shape they were placed at.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Threshold" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Door, gate, secret door, portcullis, rubble, curtain, ladder, portal.
          Crosses a boundary when a condition holds &mdash; the only type whose
          runtime state has to persist.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Fixture">
        <p>
          Chest, lever, pressure plate, altar, brazier, workbench, bed. Sits on
          the floor or against a wall and can be operated. A chest is a fixture
          with contents; a lever is a fixture that opens a threshold.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Hazard">
        <p>
          Spikes, flame vent, acid pool, collapsing floor, dart wall. Passable
          or not, but it costs something. Its trigger is data: proximity,
          weight, timer, or a linked lever.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Resource" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Herb, ore vein, mushroom, fountain, tree. Harvestable. Respawn is
          per-instance, so "already picked" is instance state, not layout.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Spawn" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          A point that creates actors at runtime: mob, boss, NPC, captive,
          patrol. The species is a definition reference. The mobs themselves are
          never part of the layout.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Item" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Loose drop, key, quest object. A portable reference sitting on the
          floor or inside a container. Keys are items that satisfy a threshold
          condition.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Marker">
        <p>
          Entry, exit, patrol node, trigger volume, arena bounds, light, sound.
          Invisible authoring data. Occupies a point and can share space with
          anything.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="A placement is a transform">
    <pre style="margin-bottom:16px"><code>{placement}</code></pre>
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="guess" title="Snapping is editor behaviour, not schema">
        <p>
          The snap decides which number lands in <code>pos</code> and
          <code>rot</code>. A dungeon saved by a modded editor at 17&deg; is
          still a valid dungeon, because nothing downstream expects a grid.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Attachments parent, they don't copy coordinates">
        <p>
          A torch on a wall stores the wall's placement id &mdash; move the wall,
          the torch follows. Two independent coordinate pairs for the same
          physical relationship always drift eventually.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Physics uses the transformed hull">
        <p>
          The same small convex proxy, rotated by whatever the author chose. The
          hex never enters the simulation, so a crate at 30&deg; collides at
          30&deg; and nothing has to be re-snapped to load a map.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Multi-level falls out for free">
        <p>
          Because <code>z</code> is just a coordinate, a bridge over a pit is two
          surfaces in one column with no special case &mdash; which was the
          thing the cell model could not express.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Snap targets">
    <pre style="margin-bottom:16px"><code>{snapTargets}</code></pre>
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="guess" title="The target follows the block class">
        <p>
          Walls want edges, crates want centres, posts want corners. One global
          snap setting would put a door in the middle of a room.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Rotation snaps separately from position">
        <p>
          A wall on an edge has no rotation to choose: the edge decides it. A
          crate on a centre is free to face any of the 30&deg; steps. The two
          snaps are independent, and either can be off.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Free placement is a real mode">
        <p>
          Set pieces &mdash; a throne, a collapsed arch &mdash; should not be
          forced onto a hex. Leaving the grid is cheaper than teaching the grid
          every exception.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Hexes are still the analysis unit">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="guess" title="Project, don't store">
        <p>
          When something needs a cell &mdash; region queries, occupancy, spawn
          checks &mdash; project the placement's position onto the hex under it.
          The projection is a question, never an identity.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="The connectivity graph is derived at publish">
        <p>
          Nothing is stored as edges any more, so pathfinding input is computed:
          rasterise walls and doors onto hex boundaries, flood-fill the rest.
          That is the same graph the previous model stored directly, rebuilt from
          the scene.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Rotated things straddle hexes">
        <p>
          A crate at 30&deg; overlaps two cells. Assign it by its anchor and say
          so once, or the region queries and the art will disagree about which
          room it is in.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="The projection is disposable">
        <p>
          It can be thrown away and rebuilt from placements plus block
          definitions. Store it only because rebuilding on every join would make
          load time a function of map size.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Gates and versions">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="decided" title="Gates are not special" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          A gate is a threshold whose condition is unsatisfied at spawn. Nothing
          about the object changes &mdash; only whether it opens, and what has to
          happen first.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Conditions are data; state is instance">
        <p>
          Lever, key, event (boss dead), timer, party size, puzzle. Which one is
          a field on the block. Whether it is open is a row in the instance
          delta, keyed by placement id, so it survives a restart and dies with
          the instance.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Publishing freezes a version">
        <p>
          Hand-built maps get edited forever, and an instance may be mid-run
          while that happens. An instance pins a
          <code>blueprint_version</code>; the master file keeps moving.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Old versions are never deleted">
        <p>
          The same rule as everywhere else. A finished run still points at the
          exact arrangement it happened in, which is the only way to answer "what
          did they actually walk through?"
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Hand-authored first">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="guess" title="The editor ships, because the base is hand-built">
        <p>
          Players build their own dungeon, so this is not a staff tool. Snap,
          undo, validation and copy-paste are gameplay features &mdash; and they
          have to work on a phone, which is where hand-placing a wall gets
          interesting.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="No generator contract, so ports are optional">
        <p>
          Fragments can still be pasted and aligned, but nothing has to satisfy a
          placer. Ports become alignment labels instead of a compatibility proof,
          which is much less to get wrong.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Validation moves to publish">
        <p>
          Entry reachable, exit reachable, no orphan region, every gate solvable.
          Checked when the author saves, with the author looking at it &mdash;
          not when a seed is rolled at three in the morning.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Procedural isn't cancelled, it's deferred">
        <p>
          A placement list is exactly what a generator would emit. Keeping the
          format the same means the generator can be added without a migration,
          and hybrid maps can mix authored fragments into generated shells.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Players fork maps, they don't generate them">
        <p>
          The interesting reuse for hand-built content is copy, paste and edit.
          That needs fragments and versions far more than it needs a room type.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Database shape">
    <Card>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li><b>blueprints</b> &mdash; name, owner, current version pointer. The thing that keeps moving.</li>
        <li><b>blueprint_versions</b> &mdash; immutable snapshots. What an instance actually pins.</li>
        <li><b>placements</b> &mdash; version, block, position, rotation, parent, params.</li>
        <li><b>fragments</b> &mdash; a reusable group of placements, pasted with a transform.</li>
        <li><b>derived_regions</b> &mdash; version, hex, region id. Projection, rebuildable.</li>
        <li><b>instance.state</b> &mdash; keyed by placement id: gate open, chest taken, spawn cleared.</li>
      </ul>
    </Card>
    <div class="grid" style="margin-top:16px">
      <Card status="guess" title="Transforms are rows, not a blob">
        <p>
          "Every gate this lever opens" and "every chest in this version" become
          queries. A serialised array is smaller and useless for exactly the
          questions the editor and the validator ask.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="A version is the unit of immutability">
        <p>
          Blueprints churn; versions never do. Everything that has to be stable
          &mdash; instance state, run records, screenshots in a dispute &mdash;
          points at a version and can trust its ids.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Retired, not deleted, again">
        <p>
          A block or fragment that falls out of use gets <code>retired_at</code>.
          Old versions still render, and old instances still validate, because
          nothing they point at has vanished.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Open questions">
    <Card>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>
          <b>What is the anchor rule for straddling objects?</b> Anchor point,
          centre of mass, or the hex containing most of the hull. One sentence,
          applied everywhere, or the projection disagrees with what the player
          sees.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>How big is a hex?</b> It no longer constrains physics, only how
          coarse placement feels. Two metres is finer than most set pieces need;
          four metres makes a door snap feel like a decision.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>30&deg; everywhere, or only for fixtures?</b> Storage allows any
          angle. The question is what the editor offers, and whether a wall is
          ever allowed off its edge.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Can a fragment be pasted off-grid?</b> If a saved arrangement only
          lands on a hex origin, combining two authored maps stays tidy. If it
          lands anywhere, the seams are the author's problem.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Does the editor need a rebuild-graph button?</b> Deriving at publish
          is fine until someone edits a map they are standing in. Decide whether
          that is blocked, warned, or hot-swapped on next entry.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Card>
  </Section>
</Base>
